    <h2>Repository projects</h2>

    <table class="docs-table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Project</th>
                <th>Responsibility in this snapshot</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>
                    <code>Crafty</code>
                </td>
                <td>The game executable: player, gameplay systems, HUD, content bootstrap, chunk jobs, and assets.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>
                    <code>CraftyNative</code>
                </td>
                <td>Runtime: ECS, scenes, input/window integration, rendering, UI framework, HUD widgets, sky, animation, and physics helpers.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>
                    <code>Crafty.SDK</code>
                </td>
                <td>Public mod-facing contracts, including <code>IMod</code>, <code>IModContext</code>, block/item definitions, and world-generation interfaces.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>
                    <code>Crafty.ChunkGeneration</code>
                </td>
                <td>World/chunk implementation, generation, and persistence code.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>
                    <code>Crafty.GameManagement</code> / <code>Crafty.InstancesManager</code>
                </td>
                <td>Launcher-side service and instance-management libraries; parts remain stubs or incomplete.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>
                    <code>Crafty.MDK</code>
                </td>
                <td>Placeholder project in this snapshot; it is not yet a working mod template.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>
                    <code>Crafty.Testing</code>
                </td>
                <td>Console test harness; the repository README notes that its current calls are out of date.</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>How to read these docs</h2>
    <p>Engine pages explain code that exists. SDK pages describe public types that exist, and explicitly call out where a public contract is not yet connected to a runtime feature. Examples are written against the checked-in signatures; they are not promises of a complete external mod-loading workflow.</p>

